concept :the_letter_match do
  name "The Letter Match"
  tags :legibility, :governance, :trade
  prominence :marginal

  prose <<~PROSE
    Establishing what an unmarked piece of cast metal is by matching its letters. A letter on a printed fee line or a cast bell inscription comes off a separate piece of metal, one letter to a shank, and a set of those wears unevenly: a serif broken from the R, a counter filled on the O, a corner gone from the foot of the 4. A set of letters therefore identifies a set of stamps the way a hand identifies a writer, and the match is made letter by letter against a book of impressions rather than by reading the words.

    The practice settles two kinds of question. A fee at a #{ref :the_gatekeepers, "Gatekeeper"} crossing, where the party at the desk disputes not the amount but whether the line was ever set at that station; and the identity of a cast bell whose founder's mark has been scoured off the crown.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :mechanics, heading: "The Book"
    A match needs impressions taken from the stamps themselves, on a stated interval, and initialled by somebody who was present. #{ref :the_sort_book, "The sort book"} at #{ref :yumen_gate, "Yumen Gate"} is pulled on the first working day of each month from the station's own fount, which is why a Gatekeeper fee dispute can be closed in an afternoon.

    A ringing band's book is a set of rubbings taken off the bells rather than impressions taken off stamps, one remove worse and usually all there is. A rubbing carries whatever tuning, scouring and corrosion have done to the letters since the casting, so a match made against one is argued over a handful of surviving letters rather than over an alphabet.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :limits, heading: "What A Match Dates"
    A set of stamps outlives the person who cut it. Stamps are inherited, sold, lent and copied, and the same alphabet appears on work fifty years apart in the hands of two successors and a rival. A match therefore establishes which stamps and never which year.

    #{ref :caretaker, "Caretaker"}'s asset schedule holds one field for the age of a thing and enters a matched inscription into it as a date of manufacture. #{ref :the_sixth_bell_finding, "The sixth-bell finding"} at #{ref :ste_anne_belfry, "the Ste. Anne belfry"} is where that field was filled in with nothing.
  PROSE

  log "2026-08-09 — Campanological attribution decides the method and its defect. Down to about the end of the seventeenth century each letter of a bell inscription was set into the mould from a separate tablet of metal, and bells with no founder's mark are attributed by comparing their alphabets against catalogued founders' alphabets — a comparative study of inscriptions and stamps rather than of texts. The standing caveat is that bells continued to carry a founder's name and letters after his death, so an alphabet dates the stamps and not the casting. Borrowed: letter-by-letter comparison against a book of impressions, uneven wear as the identifying feature, and the liability that a match cannot state a year. Reason: the two authorities in this world that rest on a physical object two parties can both hold — a tray of type and a ring of bells — are argued with the same evidence, and that evidence has the same defect in both."
end
